# Task 4: Difference Between PUT and PATCH Methods


## 1. Difference Between PUT and PATCH

| Point | PUT | PATCH |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Replaces or updates a complete resource | Updates selected fields of a resource |
| Data sent | Usually the complete resource | Only the fields that need to change |
| Example | Updating the entire user profile | Updating only the phone number |
| Request body | Full profile details | Partial profile details |
| HTTP method | PUT | PATCH |

## 2. Real-World Example

Suppose a food delivery app has this user profile:

```json
{
  "name": "Kirti",
  "email": "kirti@example.com",
  "phone": "9876543210"
}
```

### Example A: PUT Request

Use PUT when you want to replace the complete profile representation.

**Request:**

```http
PUT /users/1
Content-Type: application/json
```

**Request Body:**

```json
{
  "name": "Kirti Dobariya",
  "email": "kirti@example.com",
  "phone": "9123456780"
}
```

**Explanation:** The request sends the complete profile with the updated name and phone number.

Under standard replacement semantics, fields omitted from the request may be removed or reset, depending on the API.

### Example B: PATCH Request

Use PATCH when you want to update only one field.

**Request:**

```http
PATCH /users/1
Content-Type: application/json
```

**Request Body:**

```json
{
  "phone": "9123456780"
}
```

**Explanation:** Only the phone number is being updated. The name and email are intended to remain unchanged.

Note: `/users/1` is an illustrative endpoint, not a real food delivery API.

## 3. JavaScript Code Example

### PUT Request

```javascript
const updatedProfile = {
  name: "Kirti Dobariya",
  email: "kirti@example.com",
  phone: "9123456780"
};

fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1", {
  method: "PUT",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(updatedProfile)
})
  .then(async (response) => {
    console.log("Method: PUT");
    console.log("Status Code:", response.status);
    console.log("Response:", await response.json());
  })
  .catch((error) => console.error("Error:", error));
```

### PATCH Request

```javascript
const updatedPhone = {
  phone: "9123456780"
};

fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1", {
  method: "PATCH",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(updatedPhone)
})
  .then(async (response) => {
    console.log("Method: PATCH");
    console.log("Status Code:", response.status);
    console.log("Response:", await response.json());
  })
  .catch((error) => console.error("Error:", error));
